WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:01.680
Hi, I'm Mattie Wasiak.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.480
And I'm a junior studying
computer science.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:04.860
And my project is--

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.200
I've been looking at a data
set called Data Warehouse

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.080
Connect to try to use
it to optimize oxygen

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.370
delivery to the preterm infant.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:13.920
And so the motivation
behind this

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.800
is it's important to keep
preterm infants within a set

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.170
oxygen range because if
their oxygen dips too low

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.840
it's called hypoxemia,
which causes mortality,

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.720
and if it's too high, it's
called hyperoxemia and causes

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:25.562
morbidity.

00:00:25.562 --> 00:00:28.020
And so we have this data set
called Data Warehouse Connect.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.840
And it provides quasi-real-time
data, including alarms,

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.900
trans physiological
wave forms, SvO2 data,

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.150
access for all the patients
in the NICU at Beth Israel

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.680
Deaconess Medical Center.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.890
And so we sampled this data
set to get patients that

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.870
are preterm, which is
less than 37 weeks,

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.900
and also in the hospital
for more than 24 hours.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.730
And so this led to 367 patients.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.490
And so we looked at
the oxygen saturation

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.550
for these 367 patients to
determine that the coverage

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.550
that we had.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.450
And so we found on average we
have 96 percent data coverage

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.780
for these patients.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.630
And so this means from
the time of admission

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.005
to the time of
discharge, we have

00:01:02.005 --> 00:01:03.810
second by second
oxygen saturation

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.900
information for 96 percent
of the patient's day.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.960
And so this leads to about 8,000
patient days of oxygen data

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.048
showing that this is
a very rich data set.

00:01:12.048 --> 00:01:13.590
And so when we dive
a little bit more

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:17.250
deeper into the oxygen
saturation of the patients,

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.830
we grouped the patients
by gestational age

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:23.205
and by birth weight to see
how these two factors affected

00:01:23.205 --> 00:01:25.830
the percent time that they spent
outside of their target oxygen

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:27.030
saturation range.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.410
And so we found patterns
that you would expect to see.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.712
So we found that the most
premature patients and also

00:01:31.712 --> 00:01:33.420
the patients with the
lowest birth weight

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.210
were the most likely to leave
their target oxygen saturation

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:36.870
range.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.630
Thank you.

